The Passage
[trigger road]

You hear it before you see it, not the steady clank-whir of the big Iron, but skittering and scraping. A burst of metal on glass. Silence. A grinding lurch.

It comes around a junction box at a dead sprint, shoulder-checking the wall and throwing sparks. Horse-sized, iron body, black glass where a face should be. One foreleg cycles faster than the others. It crosses the street in a crooked diagonal, slams into a pump house, shudders, then bolts back the way it came.

Erratice and unpredictable; you can't outsmart this one. You search for an exit. Anything to get out of the monster's path. You see the hatch, heavy steel, torn open and hanging loose from a single hinge. 

Below the hatch, stairs. Concrete, utilitarian, descending into a maintenance tunnel lit by dim amber lights.

The tunnel is dry and wide enough for two abreast. Pipe runs along the ceiling. Junction markings at every intersection, the same inscrutable symbols you find everywhere here. But also someone has carved crude arrows into the walls. You follow them, perhaps someone has marked a safe path for you. A lucky break at last.

Sixty yards in you find the camp. Someone had time here. One of the walls is fire-blackened, nearby you find empty water skins, empty ration wraps, a fire kit with the striker worn to a nub. Tally marks in the wall; four days. They held this position for a while. They were organized, disciplined.

Twenty yards more, you find where the carved arrows were leading you; the tunnel ends at a sealed hatch, heavy and recessed.  They couldn't open it. They tried. There are pry marks around the frame, chisel strikes on the hinges, a shallow hole chipped into the concrete where someone tried to go around it. Diagrams andnotes carved on the wall.

They died here. Seven of them. You can see it in the way they fell; tight cluster, backs to the hatch, facing the corridor. Shield positions. They locked formation and held. The remains are sixty years dry. Leather over bone, kit corroded but intact enough to read: 17th Mountain Regiment, hard men, but no match for the Grid.

The soldier on the right flank wears better armor. Maybe an officer, maybe a decorated veteran. It won't save you any more than it saved them, but you take it anyway. It belongs with the legion, not here in this tomb.

Behind you, from the direction of the stairs, a sound. Metal on concrete. A grinding lurch, a staccato burst, then a scrape that echoes down the corridor and doesn't stop. The thing from the street found the hatch, and its coming for you.

choices:

* Stand and fight = There's no way you can outrun the monster
  The tunnel is narrow. In open ground this thing would be unstoppable. Here it has to come at you straight.
  @if check combat correct:outlast wrong:rush {
    You let it come into the narrow section and give ground each time it swings, trading space for its momentum. You take two hits that you feel through the armor and wait for it to overextend. When it does, you get inside its reach. Something you strike matters. The grinding grows worse. It staggers. The second time it staggers you push through before it recovers, and on the third stumble the legs fold and it goes down sideways with a crash that shakes dust from the ceiling.
    The tunnel is silent. You find another way out.
    +advance_time 4
    +damage_spirits 2
  } @else {
    You go at it head-on, which is the wrong instinct in a tight space. The first real hit sends you into the wall and the second takes the air from you. You scramble under it and through its legs, which works through luck more than intention, then run in the only direction left. You find a maintenance hatch forty yards down, get it open, and seal it behind you.
    +add_condition injured
    +advance_time 5
  }

* Figure out the door = Try to puzzle out the lock
  Seven people worked on this before you and none of them opened it. You have what they didn't: their diagrams, their notes carved into the wall. You read what they tried and work out what they missed.
  @if check cunning correct:scheme wrong:bluff {
    The symbols aren't a combination. They're a sequence with a deliberate skip, the same pattern used on bonded Guild warehouses to prevent unauthorized entry. You work through the logic. When you press the marks in the right order the hatch drops rather than swings, and beyond it are stairs and amber light going down. You go without looking back.
    +advance_time 2
  } @else {
    You're close, you can feel it, but the sound from the corridor is getting louder and you rush the last step and get it wrong. The hatch stays sealed.
    The maintenance vent near the floor is barely large enough, but the thing behind you isn't. You go through on hands and knees with the sound of it directly behind, and crawl until the space opens into a utility shaft. You climb until it ends at a grate that opens onto empty sky.
    +damage_spirits 2
    +advance_time 5
  }

* Find another way = There are passages you didn't explore
  The junction markings covered every branch. There have to be more ways through here than the one the Iron came in from.
  @if check bushcraft correct:reroute wrong:plan {
    You read the markings properly this time. A symbol you passed twice already marks maintenance access, branching off every twenty meters. You find the branch, follow it into a parallel tunnel running east, then a ladder, then a service hatch that opens under collapsed masonry sixty meters from where you started.
    +advance_time 3
    +damage_spirits 1
  } @else {
    Two dead ends, almost a third, before the sound catches up. You press flat into a side branch too narrow for the thing to follow and wait with your back to the wall while it fills the passage ahead, glass eye sweeping once, twice, then moving on. You stay there long enough to be certain. By the time you work back out through the route you found on the third try, the sky outside has changed.
    +advance_time 6
    +damage_spirits 3
  }
